Reproduced in a scratch copy (cp -a, .git preserved), toolchain go1.26.6.
Manifest (.swarm/manifests/X5.2.files) = loader.go + alias_stable_id_test.go,
matches `git diff --stat HEAD -- internal/services/dataloader/` exactly (only
loader.go modified; the test file is the only untracked dataloader file
besides my own scratch probes) -- no undeclared touch.

Fix mechanism verified by reading the code, not just re-running tests:
SaveAlias now calls rekeyToStable(aliases, dl.stableIDIndex()) BEFORE
computing key := dl.canonicalKey(hash) and before the set/delete, so the
map is already canonical (StableID wins, at most one entry per row) by the
time the caller's own key is resolved and applied. `git diff HEAD --
loader.go` shows this is a structural reorder, not a patch on top of
attempt 1's guard: the old `if key != hash { delete(aliases, hash) }`
partial guard is gone entirely, replaced by the unconditional
normalize-then-mutate sequence. Locking pattern (`tx, done :=
dl.beginWrite(); defer done()`) is byte-identical to before -- confirmed
unchanged.

Attempt-1 defect, re-attacked and confirmed dead:
- Exact attempt-1 probe rerun: {hash: "Legacy", stableID: "Stable name"},
  SaveAlias(stableID, "") -> map EMPTY, and stays empty after a LoadData
  reload cycle (TestProbeSecond_ResurrectRerun). Traced by hand too:
  rekeyToStable sees the legacy hash resolves to the taken stable slot,
  drops "Legacy" (StableID wins), leaving {stableID: "Stable name"}; then
  canonicalKey(stableID) is a no-op and the delete lands on that same key
  -> {}.
- Order-of-operations edges, both directions:
  - {hash: "Legacy"} only + SaveAlias(stableID, "") -> normalize creates
    the stable slot first, delete still lands on it -> empty
    (TestProbeSecond_RemoveByStableWithOnlyLegacyStaged).
  - {stableID: "Stable name"} only + SaveAlias(hash, "") -> canonicalKey
    resolves hash to stable, delete lands correctly -> empty
    (TestProbeSecond_RemoveByHashWithOnlyStableStaged).
- Unresolvable-key preservation under a REMOVAL specifically (oracle only
  checks preservation under an add): {orphan, stableID} staged, remove by
  hash -> orphan survives, exactly 1 entry left
  (TestProbeSecond_UnresolvableKeyPreservedUnderRemoval). Independently
  reconfirms TestOracleX5UnresolvableLegacyKeyPreserved's add-side case too.
- SaveAlias("", "") -> no "" key materializes, 0 entries.
  SaveAlias("", "orphan-name") -> "" key DOES materialize (same as
  attempt-1's tolerated behavior) but stays inert: a subsequent LoadData
  never applies it to the real row, matching ResolveByIdentity's non-empty
  key guards (TestProbeSecond_EmptyKeySaves). No regression from attempt 1.
- Concurrent hammer: 20 goroutines alternating SaveAlias(hash/stableID,
  name-i) with 20 concurrent LoadData calls, `-race`, no data race, final
  state has at most 1 surviving entry, always filed under hash or stableID
  (TestProbeSecond_ConcurrentSaveAliasLoadDataHammer). Locking claim holds.

Novel finding, investigated and ruled NOT a defect in this task (documented
for the record, not held against the verdict): rekeyToStable's own
collision-resolution loop is order-dependent when TWO DIFFERENT legacy hash
keys in the same map both resolve, via the index, to the SAME StableID --
which of the two colliding values survives depends on Go's randomized map
iteration order (confirmed nondeterministic: 173/27 split over 200 runs
calling the unexported rekeyToStable directly with a hand-built {hashA,
hashB} -> same-stableID index). Traced whether this is reachable through
SaveAlias's real code path and it is not: all 4 production call sites
(loader.go, amazon_enrichment.go, transaction_pins.go feed
dl.stableIDIndex(); duplicate_decisions.go feeds a different pair-key
index) read only dl.stableByHash, which is written exclusively by
`dl.setStableIndex(nil)` or `dl.setStableIndex(assignStableIDs(allTransactions))`
-- never merged or accumulated across loads. assignStableIDs assigns each
row's StableID from an occurrence counter keyed on (account-slot, date,
cents) that increments per row processed regardless of hash, so two rows
with DIFFERENT hashes in the same load structurally cannot land on the same
StableID, and same-hash exact duplicates collapse to one index entry
("first occurrence wins", documented in assignStableIDs' own comment). The
index rekeyToStable is actually fed in production is therefore provably
injective on Hash, so the ambiguous-winner precondition can never arise
through SaveAlias, applyAliases, or LoadData -- only through directly
calling the unexported helper with an index no loader code can produce.
Per the bar the attempt-1 FAIL itself used ("reachable through the
documented contract exactly as the method's own docstring says it should
work"), this does not clear that bar and is not grounds for FAIL here.

